Goal:
A cron job running on the system executes a script as bandit23 that 
places the password for the next level into a file in /tmp.

Connect:
ssh bandit22@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

Approach:
. Checked /etc/cron.d/ for scheduled jobs and found one running as bandit23.
. Read the referenced script to see what it does.
. The script computes an MD5 hash of a specific string and copies 
  bandit23's password into a file in /tmp named after that hash.
. Reproduced the same MD5 hash locally to identify the exact filename, 
  then read that file.

Command:
cat /etc/cron.d/cronjob_bandit22
cat /usr/bin/cronjob_bandit22.sh
echo I am user bandit23 | md5sum | cut -d ' ' -f 1
cat /tmp/<resulting_md5_hash>

Breakdown:
cat /etc/cron.d/cronjob_bandit22        -> shows the cron job runs a script every minute as bandit23
cat /usr/bin/cronjob_bandit22.sh        -> reveals the script logic: it hashes a string and copies 
                                            bandit23's password into /tmp using that hash as the filename
echo ... | md5sum | cut -d ' ' -f 1     -> recreates the same MD5 hash used by the script, giving 
                                            the exact filename in /tmp
cat /tmp/<hash>                          -> reads the file, which contains bandit23's password

Result:
The command outputs bandit23's password, allowing login to the next level.

Key Takeaway:
Cron jobs running as another user can leak sensitive files if their 
scripts are world-readable. Inspecting scheduled tasks and the scripts 
they call can reveal predictable file paths or naming schemes, even 
when the output itself isn't directly visible.


